window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"id": "mental-model", "url": "mental-model.html", "title": "Mental model", "text": "01 mental model git stores snapshots , not diffs. every commit is a full picture of your project, identified by a hash, pointing back to its parent(s). branches are just movable labels on commits. working directory files you edit staging area \u201cindex\u201d \u2014 next commit repository .git \u2014 commit history git add git commit git restore --staged git restore git switch / git checkout commit an immutable snapshot plus metadata (author, message, parent hashes). identified by a sha like a1b2c3d . branch a lightweight pointer to a commit. committing moves the current branch forward automatically. head \u201cwhere you are now.\u201d usually points to a branch; if it points straight at a commit, you\u2019re in detached head . remote another copy of the repo (e.g. on github). origin/main is your local memory of where main is on origin ."}, {"id": "setup", "url": "setup.html", "title": "Setup & config", "text": "02 setup & config config lives at three levels: --system , --global (your user), and --local (this repo). more specific wins. identity stamped on every commit you make. git config --global user.name \"your name\" git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" sensible defaults modern settings worth turning on. git config --global init.defaultbranch main git config --global pull.rebase true git config --global push.autosetupremote true git config --global rerere.enabled true git config --global core.editor \"code --wait\" aliases shortcuts for commands you type constantly. git config --global alias.st \"status -sb\" git config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --all --decorate\" git config --global alias.undo \"reset --soft head~1\" inspect config see every value and which file set it. git config --list --show-origin git config --global --edit ssh key for github authenticate without passwords. ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -c \"you@example.com\" cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub # paste into github \u2192 settings \u2192 ssh keys ssh -t git@github.com sign commits show a \u201cverified\u201d badge using your ssh key. git config --global gpg.format ssh git config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub git config --global commit.gpgsign true"}, {"id": "starting", "url": "starting.html", "title": "Starting a repo", "text": "03 starting a repo create a new repo turn the current directory into a git repository. git init git init my-project # create a new folder clone an existing repo download a full copy including history. git clone https://github.com/user/repo.git git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git my-dir git clone --depth 1 <url> # shallow: latest commit only git clone --filter=blob:none <url> # partial: fetch file contents lazily"}, {"id": "snapshots", "url": "snapshots.html", "title": "Staging & committing", "text": "04 staging & committing the staging area lets you craft exactly what goes into each commit \u2014 even individual lines of a file. see what changed status overview and line-level diffs. git status -sb git diff # unstaged changes git diff --staged # what will be committed git diff --word-diff # inline word changes stage changes choose what goes in the next commit. git add file.txt git add . # everything in this dir git add -p # pick hunks interactively git add -u # tracked files only commit record the staged snapshot. git commit -m \"add login form\" git commit -am \"fix typo\" # stage tracked + commit git commit --amend # edit the last commit git commit --amend --no-edit move & delete files let git track renames and removals. git mv old.js new.js git rm file.txt git rm --cached secrets.env # untrack, keep on disk good commit messages short imperative summary (\u226450 chars), blank line, then the why . \u201cfix race in session refresh\u201d beats \u201cfixed stuff\u201d."}, {"id": "inspecting", "url": "inspecting.html", "title": "Inspecting history", "text": "05 inspecting history browse the log many ways to slice history. git log --oneline --graph --all git log -p path/to/file # patches for one file git log --since=\"2 weeks ago\" --author=\"ana\" git log main..feature # on feature, not main git log --follow file.js # across renames search history (pickaxe) find when a string was added or removed. git log -s \"apikey\" # count of string changed git log -g \"fetch\\(.*\\)\" # regex in diff git log --grep=\"hotfix\" # commit messages look at a commit inspect any object. git show a1b2c3d git show head~2:src/app.js # file at that commit git show --stat head who changed this line? line-by-line authorship. git blame app.js git blame -l 40,60 app.js git blame -w -c app.js # ignore whitespace, detect moves search the code fast grep over tracked files. git grep \"todo\" git grep -n \"usestate\" -- '*.tsx' git grep \"old_api\" v1.0 # at a tag stats who contributes and how much. git shortlog -sn git diff --stat main...feature git count-objects -vh"}, {"id": "branching", "url": "branching.html", "title": "Branching", "text": "06 branching branches are cheap \u2014 a 41-byte file. make one for every feature, fix or experiment. main feature \u2190 head a feature branch diverging from main . create & switch switch is the modern, focused alternative to checkout . git switch -c feature/login # create + switch git switch main git switch - # previous branch git switch -c fix origin/fix # track a remote branch list branches local, remote, merged or stale. git branch -vv # with upstream + last commit git branch -a # include remotes git branch --merged main git branch --sort=-committerdate rename & delete clean up when you\u2019re done. git branch -m old-name new-name git branch -d feature # safe: only if merged git branch -d feature # force git push origin --delete feature detached head look around an old commit without a branch. git switch --detach v1.2.0 # made commits? keep them: git switch -c rescue-branch"}, {"id": "merging", "url": "merging.html", "title": "Merging & rebasing", "text": "07 merging & rebasing two ways to integrate work. merge preserves history exactly as it happened. rebase replays your commits on top of another branch for a linear history. git merge feature merge commit git rebase main replayed (new hashes) merge combine another branch into the current one. git switch main git merge feature git merge --no-ff feature # always create merge commit git merge --squash feature # one combined change git merge --abort rebase move your branch to start from a newer base. git switch feature git rebase main git rebase --continue # after fixing conflicts git rebase --abort git rebase --onto main old-base feature resolve conflicts edit the <<<<<<< markers, then stage. git status # see conflicted files git checkout --ours file.txt # keep our version git checkout --theirs file.txt # keep their version git add file.txt git mergetool better conflict markers show the common ancestor too \u2014 far easier to reason about. git config --global merge.conflictstyle zdiff3 git config --global rerere.enabled true # remember resolutions the golden rule of rebasing never rebase commits that others have already pulled. rebasing rewrites hashes; shared history should only move forward."}, {"id": "remotes", "url": "remotes.html", "title": "Remotes & collaboration", "text": "08 remotes & collaboration manage remotes named urls for other copies of the repo. git remote -v git remote add origin git@github.com:me/repo.git git remote add upstream git@github.com:org/repo.git git remote set-url origin <new-url> git remote prune origin fetch vs pull fetch downloads; pull = fetch + merge (or rebase). git fetch --all --prune git pull # fetch + integrate git pull --rebase git pull --ff-only # refuse if not fast-forward push publish your commits. git push -u origin feature # set upstream git push git push --force-with-lease # safe force push git push --tags keep a fork in sync pull upstream changes into your fork. git fetch upstream git switch main git rebase upstream/main git push --force-with-lease prefer --force-with-lease over --force it refuses to overwrite the remote if someone else pushed since your last fetch."}, {"id": "undoing", "url": "undoing.html", "title": "Undoing things", "text": "09 undoing things pick the tool by what you want to undo and whether it\u2019s been pushed . i want to\u2026 command safe after push? discard edits to a file git restore file.txt \u2014 unstage a file git restore --staged file.txt \u2014 fix the last commit message / add a forgotten file git commit --amend no undo last commit, keep changes staged git reset --soft head~1 no undo last commit, keep changes unstaged git reset head~1 no throw away last commit and its changes git reset --hard head~1 no undo a pushed commit git revert <sha> yes \u2713 revert a merge commit git revert -m 1 <merge-sha> yes \u2713 delete untracked files git clean -fd (preview: -n ) \u2014 reset branch to match remote git reset --hard origin/main \u2014 restore a file from another commit git restore --source=head~3 file.txt \u2014 git reset mode moves branch staging area working dir --soft \u2713 kept kept --mixed (default) \u2713 reset kept --hard \u2713 reset reset \u26a0"}, {"id": "stash", "url": "stash.html", "title": "Stashing", "text": "10 stashing shelve uncommitted work so you can switch context, then bring it back. save & restore a stack of temporary snapshots. git stash push -m \"wip: navbar\" git stash -u # include untracked files git stash list git stash pop # apply + drop git stash apply stash@{1} # apply, keep in list advanced stash partial stashes and branching from one. git stash push -p # choose hunks git stash push -- src/api.js # specific paths git stash show -p stash@{0} git stash branch new-branch # branch from stash git stash drop / git stash clear"}, {"id": "reflog", "url": "reflog.html", "title": "Reflog: the safety net", "text": "11 reflog: the safety net git records every move of head for ~90 days. if you committed it, you can almost always get it back \u2014 even after a bad reset --hard or a deleted branch. find where you were every checkout, commit, reset, rebase. git reflog git reflog show feature git log -g --oneline recover point a branch back at the lost commit. git reset --hard head@{2} # go back 2 moves git branch recovered a1b2c3d # resurrect a branch git switch main@{yesterday} --detach undo a bad rebase orig_head remembers where you started. git reset --hard orig_head find dangling objects last resort for things never on a branch. git fsck --lost-found git fsck --unreachable | grep commit"}, {"id": "rewriting", "url": "rewriting.html", "title": "Rewriting history", "text": "12 rewriting history interactive rebase turns a messy series of commits into a clean story before you share it. $ git rebase -i head~4 pick a1b2c3d add user model # reword = change message \u00b7 squash = meld into previous \u00b7 fixup = meld, drop message fixup e4f5a6b oops, typo reword 7c8d9e0 add api endpoint edit 1f2a3b4 big commit to split drop 5c6d7e8 debug logging common recipes autosquash fixups mark a fix for an earlier commit; git places it for you. git commit --fixup=a1b2c3d git rebase -i --autosquash main split a commit mark it edit in the rebase, then: git reset head~1 git add -p && git commit -m \"part 1\" git add -p && git commit -m \"part 2\" git rebase --continue run tests on every commit rebase stops at the first failure. git rebase -i --exec \"npm test\" main purge a file from all history use git-filter-repo (the recommended replacement for filter-branch ). pip install git-filter-repo git filter-repo --path secrets.env --invert-paths git filter-repo --replace-text expressions.txt leaked a secret? rewriting history isn\u2019t enough \u2014 anyone may have cloned it. rotate the credential first, then clean the history."}, {"id": "cherry", "url": "cherry.html", "title": "Cherry-pick & patches", "text": "13 cherry-pick & patches cherry-pick copy specific commits onto the current branch. git cherry-pick a1b2c3d git cherry-pick a1b2c3d..f6e5d4c # range git cherry-pick -x a1b2c3d # note origin in message git cherry-pick --no-commit a1b2c3d patches by email / file share commits without a shared remote. git format-patch -3 # last 3 commits \u2192 .patch files git am *.patch # apply as commits git diff > change.diff git apply change.diff # apply as unstaged changes compare two versions of a branch see what changed between rebases of a pr. git range-diff main feature@{1} feature"}, {"id": "bisect", "url": "bisect.html", "title": "Bisect", "text": "14 bisect binary-search your history to find the exact commit that introduced a bug. 1,000 commits \u2192 about 10 steps. manual git checks out the midpoint; you test and report. git bisect start git bisect bad # current commit is broken git bisect good v2.1.0 # this one worked # ...test, then: git bisect good # or: git bisect bad git bisect reset automated let a script decide \u2014 exit 0 = good, non-zero = bad. git bisect start head v2.1.0 git bisect run npm test git bisect skip # if a commit can't be tested git bisect log"}, {"id": "worktree", "url": "worktree.html", "title": "Worktrees", "text": "15 worktrees check out several branches at once in separate folders, sharing one repository. no more stashing to review a pr or ship a hotfix. git worktree add ../hotfix main # existing branch git worktree add -b review ../review origin/pr-42 git worktree list git worktree remove ../hotfix git worktree prune"}, {"id": "tags", "url": "tags.html", "title": "Tags & releases", "text": "16 tags & releases create tags annotated tags store author, date and message \u2014 use them for releases. git tag -a v1.0.0 -m \"first stable release\" git tag v1.0.0-rc1 # lightweight git tag -a v0.9.0 a1b2c3d # tag an old commit git push origin v1.0.0 use tags list, describe and package. git tag -l \"v1.*\" git describe --tags # v1.0.0-14-ga1b2c3d git tag -d v1.0.0 && git push origin :refs/tags/v1.0.0 git archive --format=zip v1.0.0 > release.zip"}, {"id": "submodules", "url": "submodules.html", "title": "Submodules & sparse", "text": "17 submodules, sparse checkout & lfs submodules embed another repo at a pinned commit. git submodule add https://github.com/lib/x libs/x git clone --recurse-submodules <url> git submodule update --init --recursive git submodule update --remote sparse checkout only materialize the folders you need in a huge monorepo. git clone --filter=blob:none --sparse <url> git sparse-checkout set apps/web packages/ui git sparse-checkout add docs git sparse-checkout disable large file storage keep big binaries out of the object database. git lfs install git lfs track \"*.psd\" git add .gitattributes git lfs ls-files speed up big repos background maintenance and filesystem monitoring. git maintenance start git config core.fsmonitor true git gc --aggressive"}, {"id": "hooks", "url": "hooks.html", "title": "Hooks", "text": "18 hooks scripts in .git/hooks/ that run on events. exit non-zero to block the action. hook runs typical use pre-commit before a commit is created lint, format, block secrets commit-msg after message is written enforce conventional commits pre-push before pushing run test suite post-checkout after switching branches install deps, regenerate files pre-receive on the server enforce policy on pushes # .githooks/pre-commit \u2014 share hooks via the repo #!/bin/sh npm run lint || exit 1 $ chmod +x .githooks/pre-commit $ git config core.hookspath .githooks"}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Common problems", "text": "19 common problems the error messages you\u2019ll actually hit, what causes them, and how to fix them. search for words from your error to jump straight to it. pushing & pulling push rejected: remote has new work ! [rejected] main -> main (fetch first) updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not have locally. why: someone pushed since your last pull. integrate their commits first, then push again. git pull --rebase git push no upstream branch fatal: the current branch feature has no upstream branch. why: a new local branch doesn\u2019t know which remote branch to push to yet. git push -u origin head # never see it again: git config --global push.autosetupremote true divergent branches on pull hint: you have divergent branches and need to specify how to reconcile them. why: both you and the remote have new commits, and git wants you to choose merge or rebase. git pull --rebase # linear history git pull --no-rebase # merge commit git config --global pull.rebase true # set a default refusing to merge unrelated histories fatal: refusing to merge unrelated histories why: you ran git init locally and the github repo was created with its own readme/license commit. the two have no common ancestor. git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories src refspec does not match any error: src refspec main does not match any why: there are no commits yet, or your branch is called master , not main . git branch --show-current # what is it called? git commit -m \"initial commit\" # if nothing committed git branch -m main git push -u origin main file too large for github remote: error: file big.zip is 142 mb; this exceeds github's file size limit of 100.00 mb why: the file is in a commit, so deleting it in a new commit isn\u2019t enough. remove it from history. # in the latest commit: git rm --cached big.zip && git commit --amend --no-edit # in older commits: git filter-repo --path big.zip --invert-paths # or move it to lfs: git lfs migrate import --include=\"*.zip\" authentication password authentication removed remote: support for password authentication was removed. fatal: authentication failed for 'https://github.com/\u2026' why: github no longer accepts account passwords over https. use the github cli, a personal access token, or ssh. gh auth login gh auth setup-git # use gh as credential helper permission denied (publickey) git@github.com: permission denied (publickey). fatal: could not read from remote repository. why: no ssh key is loaded, or the key isn\u2019t added to your github account. ssh -t git@github.com # test the connection ssh-add -l # loaded keys ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 git remote -v # ssh or https url? repository not found remote: repository not found. fatal: repository 'https://github.com/\u2026' not found why: a typo in the url, or the repo is private and you\u2019re signed in as an account without access. git remote -v gh auth status git remote set-url origin git@github.com:owner/repo.git branches & commits committed to the wrong branch you made commits on main that belong on a new feature branch, and haven\u2019t pushed them. git branch feature # save commits on a new branch git reset --hard origin/main # put main back git switch feature commit belongs on an existing branch instead? git switch right-branch git cherry-pick <sha> git switch - && git reset --hard head~1 local changes would be overwritten error: your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by checkout why: you have uncommitted edits to files the other branch changes. put them somewhere safe first. git stash -u && git switch other && git stash pop # or bring the changes along / commit them: git switch -c new-branch \u201cyou are in detached head state\u201d you are in 'detached head' state. you can look around, make experimental changes\u2026 why: you checked out a commit or tag rather than a branch. it\u2019s harmless, but new commits won\u2019t belong to any branch. git switch -c keep-my-work # keep commits made here git switch main # or just go back deleted a branch by accident git branch -d prints the hash it deleted ( was a1b2c3d ). if that\u2019s scrolled away, reflog has it. git reflog | grep feature git branch feature a1b2c3d dropped or lost a stash stashes are commits, so they linger as dangling objects until garbage collection. git fsck --no-reflog | awk '/dangling commit/ {print $3}' \\ | xargs git log --no-walk --oneline --grep=wip git stash apply <sha> wrong name or email on commits fix your config, then rewrite the affected (unpushed) commits. git config user.email \"right@example.com\" git commit --amend --reset-author --no-edit # last commit git rebase -r main --exec \"git commit --amend --reset-author --no-edit\" undo a pushed merge on main don\u2019t reset shared history. revert it, keeping parent 1 ( main ) as the mainline. git log --merges --oneline -5 git revert -m 1 <merge-sha> git push need to bypass a hook a pre-commit or pre-push hook is blocking an urgent fix. use sparingly. git commit --no-verify -m \"hotfix\" git push --no-verify stuck states stuck mid-merge or mid-rebase error: you have not concluded your merge (merge_head exists). why: a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stopped on a conflict. git status says which one; finish it or abort. git status git merge --abort # or --continue git rebase --abort # or --continue / --skip git cherry-pick --abort index.lock exists fatal: unable to create '.git/index.lock': file exists. why: another git process (often your editor) is running, or one crashed and left the lock behind. make sure nothing is running, then remove it. ps aux | grep -i git rm .git/index.lock not a git repository fatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git why: you\u2019re in the wrong folder, or the repo hasn\u2019t been initialised. pwd git rev-parse --show-toplevel # where's the repo root? git init # if it's meant to be new editor opened and you can\u2019t get out git opened vim for a commit or merge message. type :wq and enter to save, or :cq to abort. then pick a friendlier editor: git config --global core.editor \"nano\" git config --global core.editor \"code --wait\" files & diffs .gitignore isn\u2019t ignoring a file why: .gitignore only affects untracked files. if it was committed before, git keeps tracking it. git rm --cached .env # or -r for a folder git commit -m \"stop tracking .env\" git check-ignore -v .env # confirm the rule matches whole file shows as changed why: usually line endings (crlf vs lf) differing between windows and macos/linux. echo \"* text=auto\" >> .gitattributes git add --renormalize . git commit -m \"normalize line endings\" git config --global core.autocrlf input # mac/linux only file permissions changed old mode 100644 new mode 100755 why: the executable bit flipped, often from copying files or using a shared drive. git config core.filemode false git diff --summary | grep mode renaming only the case of a file on macos and windows the filesystem ignores case, so readme.md \u2192 readme.md can go unnoticed. use git mv : git mv readme.md readme.md # older git: rename via a temporary name git mv readme.md tmp && git mv tmp readme.md conflict in a lock file don\u2019t hand-merge package-lock.json , yarn.lock and similar. take one side and regenerate it. git checkout --theirs package-lock.json npm install git add package-lock.json submodule folder is empty why: the repo was cloned without --recurse-submodules . git submodule update --init --recursive git config --global submodule.recurse true"}, {"id": "debugging", "url": "debugging.html", "title": "Debugging Git itself", "text": "20 debugging git itself when git does something you don\u2019t expect, make it show its work. trace what git is doing environment variables that turn on detailed logging for a single command. git_trace=1 git pull # commands & aliases run git_trace_performance=1 git status # where time goes git_trace_setup=1 git status # repo & worktree discovery network & auth problems see the http or ssh conversation with the server. git_curl_verbose=1 git_trace_curl=1 git fetch git_ssh_command=\"ssh -v\" git fetch git config --get-all credential.helper \u201cwhere does this setting come from?\u201d find which config file sets a value, and what the result is. git config --show-origin --get pull.rebase git config --list --show-scope git var -l | grep -i editor is it the alias, hook or git? rule out customisations one by one. git config --get-regexp '^alias\\.' ls .git/hooks | grep -v sample git -c core.hookspath=/dev/null commit # run without hooks git --version slow repository measure first, then tune. git count-objects -vh git maintenance run --task=gc git config core.untrackedcache true git config feature.manyfiles true check repo health verify integrity and bundle diagnostics for a bug report. git fsck --full git diagnose -o ~/desktop # zip of repo stats git bugreport # system info template read the hints git\u2019s error output usually ends with a hint: telling you the exact command to run. git status is the best first step for almost any confusing state."}, {"id": "ignore", "url": "ignore.html", "title": ".gitignore", "text": "21 .gitignore pattern syntax one pattern per line. # comments start with # node_modules/ # a directory anywhere *.log # by extension /build # only at repo root docs/**/*.pdf # any depth !important.log # re-include .env* debug & scope why is this file ignored? git check-ignore -v path/to/file git status --ignored # personal, unshared ignores: echo \"scratch/\" >> .git/info/exclude git config --global core.excludesfile ~/.gitignore_global"}, {"id": "refs", "url": "refs.html", "title": "Revision syntax", "text": "22 revision syntax ways to name a commit anywhere git expects one. syntax meaning head the current commit head~3 three commits back along first parents head^2 the second parent of a merge commit main@{upstream} / @{u} the branch\u2019s upstream (e.g. origin/main ) main@{2.days.ago} where main pointed two days ago (reflog) a..b commits in b not in a (\u201cwhat\u2019s new on b\u201d) a...b commits in either but not both; in diff , changes since the merge-base :/fix login most recent commit whose message matches v1.0:src/app.js a file\u2019s contents at a revision"}, {"id": "workflows", "url": "workflows.html", "title": "Workflows", "text": "23 workflows github flow branch off main , open a pull request, review, merge, deploy. simple and great for continuous delivery. git switch -c feature/search # ...commit work git push -u origin feature/search gh pr create --fill trunk-based everyone integrates into main at least daily via small, short-lived branches. unfinished work hides behind feature flags. git flow long-lived main + develop , with feature/ , release/ and hotfix/ branches. suits versioned, scheduled releases. conventional commits machine-readable messages that drive changelogs and semver. feat(auth): add oauth login fix(api): handle empty response docs: update readme feat!: drop node 16 support"}, {"id": "cheatsheet", "url": "cheatsheet.html", "title": "Cheat sheet", "text": "24 daily cheat sheet git switch main && git pull # start fresh git switch -c feature/thing # new branch git add -p # stage carefully git commit -m \"describe the change\" git fetch && git rebase origin/main # stay current git push -u origin feature/thing # share git lg # look around (alias) git reflog # when in doubt"}];
